 This problem is equivalent to solving $ax(x - a)^2 + bx(x - b)^2 = b(x - a)^2 + a(x - b)^2$.  Rearranging and factoring yields $ax(x - a)^2 - ax(x - b)^2 + bx(x - a)^2 - bx(x - b)^2 = 0,$
\begin{align*} ax[(x - a)^2 - (x - b)^2] + bx[(x - a)^2 - (x - b)^2] &= 0, ax(x - a - x + b)(x - a + x - b) + bx(x - a - x + b)(x - a + x - b) &= 0, a(x - b)(x - 2a + b) + bx(x - b)(x - 2a + b) &= 0. \end{align*}
We have $x - b = 0$ a solution (has a root $x = b$).  Otherwise, we have \[(a + bx)(x - 2a + b) = a(x - 2a + b) + bx(x - 2a + b) = ax - 2a^2 + ab + bx^2 - 2abx + b^2 x.\]  Multiplying through by $a$ and rearranging, $bx^2 + x(2b^2 - 2ab + a) + (ab - 2a^2) = 0.$  The discriminant of this quadratic is \begin{align*} (2b^2 - 2ab + a)^2 - 4(b)(ab - 2a^2) &= 4b^4 - 8b^3 + 12b^2 a - 8a^2 b + a^2 + (-4ab^2 + 8a^2 b)\\ &= 4b^4 - 8b^3 + 8b^2 a + a^2. \end{align*}  Factoring, $4b^4 - 8b^3 + 8b^2 a + a^2 = (2b^2 - 2b - a)^2 \ge 0,$ since $a \neq b,$ a perfect square.  Thus, there are 3 real solutions to the quadratic since the discriminant is positive, so adding the root from $x-a = 0,$ there are $\boxed{3}$ real solutions. </think> <answer> 3 </answer>

